<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Exp Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Experimental Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0022-1007</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-9538</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">4159522</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2138136</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>ELEVATED &#x003b3;-GLOBULIN AND INCREASED ANTIBODY PRODUCTION IN MICE INFECTED WITH LACTIC DEHYDROGENASE VIRUS</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Notkins</surname><given-names>Abner Louis</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Mergenhagen</surname><given-names>Stephan E.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Rizzo</surname><given-names>Anthony A.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Scheele</surname><given-names>Christina</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Waldmann</surname><given-names>Thomas A.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x1c0a7e0N0x38e95d0">From the Laboratory of Microbiology, National Institute of Dental Research, and Metabolism Service, National Cancer Institute, Bethesda, Maryland</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>31</day><month>1</month><year>1966</year></pub-date><volume>123</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>347</fpage><lpage>364</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>5</day><month>10</month><year>1965</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1966 by The Rockefeller University Press</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> Infection of mice with the lactic dehydrogenase virus (LDV) resulted in an elevated level of &#x003b3;-globulin. Histologic examination of the spleen and lymph nodes revealed that the number of germinal centers was greatly increased. Immunization with human &#x003b3;-globulin showed that the capacity of the virus-infected animal to produce anti-human &#x003b3;-globulin was greatly enhanced and that the virus acted as an adjuvant. From these experiments it is concluded that a virus infection (LDV) can affect the immunologic response of the host to a heterologous antigen. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>